sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-01> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Whatever kind of underlying rules a simple program uses (cellular automata, mobile automata, Turing machines, substitution systems, tag systems, register machines, symbolic systems), the overall behavior that emerges falls into the same few basic types first seen in elementary cellular automata.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Overall behavior types are similar across all kinds of simple programs";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “The Search for General Features” (from p. 51), p. 52. Scope: simple programs of all the kinds surveyed in chapter 3"
}
